akr 0.1
project jpegxl-rs

record jpegxl-rs.question.low-target-one-pass-parameters/1 : question {
    title "Low-target one pass: below which target, and does one pass mean one verified probe or no verification?"
    state superseded
    question """
        The operator's rule that lower targets are one pass by design (@jpegxl-rs.decision.low-targets-are-one-pass-by-design) leaves two parameters open. (1) The threshold: which requested scores count as 'lower'? The production presets are Fast 70 and Balanced 85, and the corrected stop already pays from 70 upward, so the candidates are 'below the Fast preset (< 70)' or a fixed cut such as <= 50. (2) The semantics: does one pass mean one canonical probe at the predicted rung whose stream is emitted whenever it meets the floor, whatever the overshoot (one render, one score, one exact price — the floor contract kept), or a single plan emitted without a score (no floor verification, which changes the public minimum-score contract at those targets)? A third detail follows from the answer: what happens when the single probe misses the floor — the existing bounded expansion, a one-jump rescue, or the under-target fallback semantics of @jpegxl-rs.decision.quality-miss-fallback-semantics.
        """
    resolution """
        Open: needs the operator. Once answered, the implementation is a feature-gated low-target route in solve_baseline plus the corpus gate at the low targets (30/50 and the chosen threshold).
        """
    derived_from [ @jpegxl-rs.decision.low-targets-are-one-pass-by-design ]
    supported_by [ @jpegxl-rs.evidence.contract-b-trial-holdout-seven-target-2026-09-02 ]
}

record jpegxl-rs.question.low-target-one-pass-parameters/2 : question {
    title "Low-target one pass: below which target, and does one pass mean one verified probe or no verification?"
    state resolved
    question """
        Low-target one pass: below which target, and does one pass mean one verified probe or no verification?
        """
    resolution """
        Neither parameter was needed. The operator delegated the decision ('do what you think is best for speed vs quality/size efficiency; if dropping probes for low quality encodes is not optimal and overly complex then do something else'). The low targets were slow not for want of a one-pass rule but because the crossing aim (threshold + reserve × loss) lay outside the fixed one-point accept band below Balanced 66.7 / Fast 83.3, so a landing on the aim never stopped the search (@jpegxl-rs.observation.crossing-aim-outside-accept-band-below-67-2026-09-02). The reserve-coupled band fixes that at every target with the floor kept and every probe still verified (@jpegxl-rs.decision.promote-reserve-coupled-band-2026-09-02). A literal unverified or first-probe-only pass was rejected: on photos at target 30 the predictor's first landing is 0.3-0.5 ln effective scale above the crossing, a large byte cost, not a saving (@jpegxl-rs.observation.qpv2-photo-low-target-bias-2026-09-02); the remaining low-target probes are the predictor's.
        """
    derived_from [ @jpegxl-rs.decision.low-targets-are-one-pass-by-design ]
    supersedes [ @jpegxl-rs.question.low-target-one-pass-parameters/1 ]
    supported_by [ @jpegxl-rs.evidence.contract-b-trial-holdout-seven-target-2026-09-02 ]
}

record jpegxl-rs.question.one-shot-calibration-contract-b/1 : question {
    title "Recalibrate qpv2 to raise the 4.3% one-shot stop rate?"
    state superseded
    question """
        Should the qpv2 prediction be recalibrated to raise the one-shot stop rate (currently 4/94 = 4.3% on the quality corpus at score 85 balanced)? The stop mechanism is already proven safe where it fires (N3, 2026-08-29: every stopped cell chose exactly the rung the full search would), and the shadow corpus attributes every missed stop to calibration: 9 of 13 confident cells overshot by more than the 1.0-point band (the candidate rung is a conservative upper quantile; predicted-rung/winner-rung median 1.34) and 8 of 12 in-band cells were blocked by the confidence gate. Each converted cell saves two of about 3.5 baseline pixel probes - roughly 850-900 ms on the 12 MP anchor. This can alter which rung wins and the emitted bytes, so it is a Contract B decision: promotion gates are a corpus-wide byte-delta and quality A/B screen and the operator's acceptance of the changed rung distribution; a first zero-risk step is retraining the model on the existing corpus traces and shadow-measuring the would-have-stopped rate without touching the encoder.
        """
    resolution """
        Open: decided by the operator against @jpegxl-rs.observation.probe-contract-b-shadow-2026-09-01/1; the zero-risk shadow retraining step needs no decision.
        """
    derived_from [ @jpegxl-rs.work.probe-simplification-next-stage-2026-09-01/1 ]
    supported_by [ @jpegxl-rs.observation.probe-contract-b-shadow-2026-09-01/1 ]
}

record jpegxl-rs.question.one-shot-calibration-contract-b/2 : question {
    title "Recalibrate qpv2 to raise the 4.3% one-shot stop rate?"
    state superseded
    question """
        Should the qpv2 prediction be recalibrated to raise the one-shot stop rate (currently 4/94 = 4.3% on the quality corpus at score 85 balanced)? The stop mechanism is already proven safe where it fires (N3, 2026-08-29: every stopped cell chose exactly the rung the full search would), and the shadow corpus attributes every missed stop to calibration: 9 of 13 confident cells overshot by more than the 1.0-point band (the candidate rung is a conservative upper quantile; predicted-rung/winner-rung median 1.34) and 8 of 12 in-band cells were blocked by the confidence gate. Each converted cell saves two of about 3.5 baseline pixel probes - roughly 850-900 ms on the 12 MP anchor. This can alter which rung wins and the emitted bytes, so it is a Contract B decision: promotion gates are a corpus-wide byte-delta and quality A/B screen and the operator's acceptance of the changed rung distribution; a first zero-risk step is retraining the model on the existing corpus traces and shadow-measuring the would-have-stopped rate without touching the encoder.
        """
    resolution """
        Open: decided by the operator. The zero-risk steps are done and narrow the question. The 2026-09-02 all-knot retrain (@jpegxl-rs.evidence.qpv2-current-corpus-retrain-2026-09-02) shows recalibrating the upper quantile alone cannot be promoted (blind p90 log-scale error 1.854, 85% fallback). The 2026-09-02 replay (@jpegxl-rs.observation.two-probe-corrected-stop-replay-2026-09-02) shows that trusting in-band first probes without the confidence gate converts 12/91 cells to one probe with no floor loss, and that a gated corrected stop adds a bounded second lever; the concrete Contract B candidates and their expected sizes are stated in @jpegxl-rs.assessment.two-probe-path-is-predictor-bound-2026-09-02. What remains for the operator is whether to authorise a feature-gated trial of (a) trusted in-band first probes plus a 0.5 ln-scale-gated corrected stop with one second correction, and (b) the flagged model median as the start for log2_pixels-flagged large photos, each gated by one_shot_promotion_ab.py on the corpus.
        """
    derived_from [ @jpegxl-rs.work.probe-simplification-next-stage-2026-09-01/1 ]
    supersedes [ @jpegxl-rs.question.one-shot-calibration-contract-b/1 ]
    supported_by [
        @jpegxl-rs.observation.probe-contract-b-shadow-2026-09-01,
        @jpegxl-rs.observation.two-probe-corrected-stop-replay-2026-09-02
    ]
}

record jpegxl-rs.question.one-shot-calibration-contract-b/3 : question {
    title "Recalibrate qpv2 to raise the 4.3% one-shot stop rate?"
    state superseded
    question """
        Should the qpv2 prediction be recalibrated to raise the one-shot stop rate (currently 4/94 = 4.3% on the quality corpus at score 85 balanced)? The stop mechanism is already proven safe where it fires (N3, 2026-08-29: every stopped cell chose exactly the rung the full search would), and the shadow corpus attributes every missed stop to calibration: 9 of 13 confident cells overshot by more than the 1.0-point band (the candidate rung is a conservative upper quantile; predicted-rung/winner-rung median 1.34) and 8 of 12 in-band cells were blocked by the confidence gate. Each converted cell saves two of about 3.5 baseline pixel probes - roughly 850-900 ms on the 12 MP anchor. This can alter which rung wins and the emitted bytes, so it is a Contract B decision: promotion gates are a corpus-wide byte-delta and quality A/B screen and the operator's acceptance of the changed rung distribution; a first zero-risk step is retraining the model on the existing corpus traces and shadow-measuring the would-have-stopped rate without touching the encoder.
        """
    resolution """
        Measured, awaiting the operator's promotion decision. Recalibrating the model cannot be promoted (@jpegxl-rs.evidence.qpv2-current-corpus-retrain-2026-09-02). The operator-authorised 2026-09-02 Contract B trial (@jpegxl-rs.observation.contract-b-corrected-stop-trial-2026-09-02) implemented three off-by-default features and ran the standing corpus gate on real encodes: `trusted-first-probe` (the N3 stop without its confidence gate) converts 8 two-probe cells to one probe with every stream byte-identical, so the calibration question's zero-cost answer is to drop the confidence gate rather than retrain; `corrected-stop` (which implies it) reaches 3.066 mean probes from 3.462 with zero floor violations and byte geomean 0.995; with `flagged-median-start` for large out-of-domain frames the combined arm reaches 3.022 (additive), passes the q85 gate, the seven-target holdout gate (0 floor, 0.9925) and t1/t4 identity (@jpegxl-rs.evidence.contract-b-trial-q85-gate-2026-09-02, @jpegxl-rs.evidence.contract-b-trial-holdout-seven-target-2026-09-02, @jpegxl-rs.evidence.contract-b-trial-thread-identity-2026-09-02). Caveats: slightly negative at targets 30 and 50, and 58 of 91 q85 streams change, so promotion re-baselines the deployed trace and the byte-identity matrices. What remains for the operator is whether to add `corrected-stop` and `flagged-median-start` (or only `trusted-first-probe`, which changes no bytes) to the policy crate's default features.
        """
    derived_from [ @jpegxl-rs.work.probe-simplification-next-stage-2026-09-01/1 ]
    supersedes [ @jpegxl-rs.question.one-shot-calibration-contract-b/2 ]
    supported_by [
        @jpegxl-rs.observation.contract-b-corrected-stop-trial-2026-09-02,
        @jpegxl-rs.observation.probe-contract-b-shadow-2026-09-01,
        @jpegxl-rs.observation.two-probe-corrected-stop-replay-2026-09-02
    ]
}

record jpegxl-rs.question.one-shot-calibration-contract-b/4 : question {
    title "Recalibrate qpv2 to raise the 4.3% one-shot stop rate?"
    state resolved
    question """
        Should the qpv2 prediction be recalibrated to raise the one-shot stop rate (currently 4/94 = 4.3% on the quality corpus at score 85 balanced)? The stop mechanism is already proven safe where it fires (N3, 2026-08-29: every stopped cell chose exactly the rung the full search would), and the shadow corpus attributes every missed stop to calibration: 9 of 13 confident cells overshot by more than the 1.0-point band (the candidate rung is a conservative upper quantile; predicted-rung/winner-rung median 1.34) and 8 of 12 in-band cells were blocked by the confidence gate. Each converted cell saves two of about 3.5 baseline pixel probes - roughly 850-900 ms on the 12 MP anchor. This can alter which rung wins and the emitted bytes, so it is a Contract B decision: promotion gates are a corpus-wide byte-delta and quality A/B screen and the operator's acceptance of the changed rung distribution; a first zero-risk step is retraining the model on the existing corpus traces and shadow-measuring the would-have-stopped rate without touching the encoder.
        """
    resolution """
        Resolved 2026-09-02 by the operator's promotion decision (@jpegxl-rs.decision.promote-corrected-stop-and-median-start-2026-09-02): not by recalibrating the model, which cannot be promoted (@jpegxl-rs.evidence.qpv2-current-corpus-retrain-2026-09-02), but by dropping the confidence gate on the one-shot stop (`trusted-first-probe`, byte-identical where it fires) and adding the gated corrected stop and the large-frame median start, which together take the corpus from 3.462 to 3.022 pixel probes per encode with zero floor violations and byte geomean 0.995 (@jpegxl-rs.observation.contract-b-corrected-stop-trial-2026-09-02). The one-shot stop rate at q85 is now 12/91; the remaining probes are bound by first-probe predictor accuracy (@jpegxl-rs.assessment.two-probe-path-is-predictor-bound-2026-09-02) and, at low targets, by the open low-target one-pass rule (@jpegxl-rs.question.low-target-one-pass-parameters).
        """
    derived_from [ @jpegxl-rs.work.probe-simplification-next-stage-2026-09-01/1 ]
    supersedes [ @jpegxl-rs.question.one-shot-calibration-contract-b/3 ]
    supported_by [
        @jpegxl-rs.observation.contract-b-corrected-stop-trial-2026-09-02,
        @jpegxl-rs.observation.probe-contract-b-shadow-2026-09-01,
        @jpegxl-rs.observation.two-probe-corrected-stop-replay-2026-09-02
    ]
}

record jpegxl-rs.question.promote-case-predictor-2026-09-02/1 : question {
    title "Promote the case-table crossing predictor (feature case-predictor) to the default controller?"
    state open
    scope [ path "JPXL/crates/jpxl-encode-policy/**" ]
    question """
        Feature `case-predictor` replaces the qpv2-st-1 linear knot model's first guess with the distance-weighted mean of the two nearest labelled images' oracle crossing curves (generated quality_predictor_cases.rs, tool subcommand train-cases). Offline it has a third of the knot model's error with no target bias (@jpegxl-rs.observation.case-table-first-guess-study-2026-09-02). On the never-seen holdout it holds the floor on all 493 cells, saves 0.47 probes per encode at Balanced for 1.4% fewer bytes and about 10% wall, and is neutral at Fast (@jpegxl-rs.evidence.case-predictor-blind-holdout-balanced-2026-09-02, @jpegxl-rs.evidence.case-predictor-blind-holdout-fast-2026-09-02). Against it: the strict preregistered verdict is Partial (two synthetic hue-wheel cells above the 1.15 byte tail), the holdout was seen twice before the final two fallback rules (the knot model's feature-range and fallback-width criteria applied to the table), the shipped 89-case table would contain the holdout images and so is not the measured model, photos gain little at high targets (the 12-50 MP camera photos have no close case) and lose a probe at 95 on two frames for 3.75% fewer bytes, and it changes 112 of 140 holdout streams. Should it join `default`? If yes, the promotion commit should add the feature to the policy crate's default set and note that pre-promotion byte baselines of the quality path are superseded again.
        """
    part_of [ @jpegxl-rs.work.pqc-one-shot-controller ]
    supported_by [
        @jpegxl-rs.evidence.case-predictor-blind-holdout-balanced-2026-09-02,
        @jpegxl-rs.evidence.case-predictor-blind-holdout-fast-2026-09-02,
        @jpegxl-rs.evidence.case-predictor-caldev-in-table-2026-09-02,
        @jpegxl-rs.evidence.case-predictor-thread-identity-2026-09-02,
        @jpegxl-rs.observation.case-table-first-guess-study-2026-09-02
    ]
}

record jpegxl-rs.question.route-text-lineart-via-bpg-preanalysis/1 : question {
    title "Route text/line-art content using the bpg-rs still265 preanalysis classifier?"
    state open
    scope [ path "JPXL/crates/**" ]
    question """
        JPXL's weakest content class against cjxl is synthetic text/line-art (up to +135% bytes at matched score, @jpegxl-rs.evidence.pqc-pr4-byte-neutral-2026-08-22). The operator points at existing first-party detection code: bpg-rs crates/still265/src/preanalysis.rs classifies 32x32 cells into 7 RegionClass values including TextLike (dense axis-aligned low-entropy edges, a text/line-art proxy) from cheap per-cell features (edge density, orientation entropy, direction dominance, flat ratio, chroma activity) plus a contextual saliency pass, with frame-level class_counts(). Should JPXL port or consume this preanalysis to detect screenshot/line-art frames and route them differently (modular/palette path, different effort or quantization policy)? Same-author code, so no licensing question; the open questions are the porting seam (jpxl-analysis vs a shared crate) and what the router changes (mode choice is a policy change under Contract B).
        """
    derived_from [ @jpegxl-rs.evidence.pqc-pr4-byte-neutral-2026-08-22 ]
}

record jpegxl-rs.question.single-finalist-pricing-contract-b/1 : question {
    title "Price only the coarsest feasible finalist? (1.3% rare-byte-change tail)"
    state open
    question """
        Should the Balanced quality search price only its coarsest feasible finalist instead of up to two (quality.rs solve_baseline: budget.exact_prices.clamp(1,2), keeping the smallest stream)? The shadow corpus shows the second exact price buys nothing on 75/76 two-finalist cells (the coarsest feasible finalist is also the smaller stream, median 9.3% smaller) but would have won on 1 cell (1.3%, a 700x700 synthetic at adjacent rungs where entropy training flipped the ordering by 18 bytes), so skipping it saves roughly one entropy training plus one emission per multi-finalist encode - about 100 ms on the 12 MP anchor - at the cost of a rare larger stream. This can alter emitted bytes, so it is a Contract B decision: promotion gates are the standing matched-rate and quality A/B screens and the operator's explicit acceptance of the 1.3% rare-byte-change tail.
        """
    resolution """
        Open: decided by the operator against @jpegxl-rs.observation.probe-contract-b-shadow-2026-09-01/1; not implementable under the current measurement-only work item.
        """
    derived_from [ @jpegxl-rs.work.probe-simplification-next-stage-2026-09-01/1 ]
    supported_by [ @jpegxl-rs.observation.probe-contract-b-shadow-2026-09-01/1 ]
}

record jpegxl-rs.question.which-perceptual-weight-crosses-the-cleanroom-line/1 : question {
    title "Where does the frequency weight's shape come from, now that a detection CSF is ruled out?"
    state superseded
    scope [ path "JPXL/crates/jpxl-encode-policy/src/csf.rs" ]
    question """
        Phase 6.3 established that frequency weighting drives the cover objective (butteraugli moves -16.4% to +32.4%, SSIMULACRA2 up to +4.37, against transform size's near-null) and that a Mannos-Sakrison detection CSF is the wrong curve, failing its pre-registered agreement check at 3.14x worst-bin ratio and r = 0.408. Measurement and screen agree on what the right curve looks like: far flatter at high frequency than a detection CSF, with no low-frequency dip.

        The obvious next step -- give the weight the measured shape -- crosses the line the workspace manifest draws around the butteraugli dependency: a table fitted to that metric's response makes this project's perceptual model a derivative of libjxl's rather than its own, with licence and provenance consequences. AGENTS.md section 2 makes that a decision to record before it is taken, not an implementation detail.

        Three options, in the preference order Phase 6.3 recorded:

        1. A distortion-visibility model from open literature rather than a detection CSF. Same clean-room standing as Phase 6.3, and the screen has now characterised what it must look like, so the search is well-posed rather than open-ended. Keeps the perceptual model this project's own.

        2. A two-parameter analytic shape -- low-frequency plateau plus a gentle rolloff -- whose parameters are set from the encoder's own quantization geometry (the I.2.5 default matrices, whose per-size step ratios Phase 6.2 already measured) rather than from butteraugli, then checked against the Phase 6.2 measurement the same way Phase 6.3 checked Mannos-Sakrison.

        3. Fit the measured curve, explicitly recorded as a decision that the perceptual model becomes libjxl-derived.

        A fourth possibility the rate split raises: the 1 bpp arm is already a uniform SSIMULACRA2 win and a 5-of-7 butteraugli win, and the 2 bpp arm is where it fails. That is evidence the correct weight interacts with which coefficients survive quantization -- something no static table can capture, and a pointer at HfQuantizer::choose, which is still nearest-reconstruction rather than RD-optimal. The next round may belong on the quantizer rather than on a better table.
        """
    blocks [ @jpegxl-rs.work.arch-phase6-4-chroma-controlled-operating-point ]
    derived_from [
        @jpegxl-rs.observation.frequency-is-the-live-lever-csf-is-the-wrong-curve-2026-08-12
    ]
    part_of [ @jpegxl-rs.track.encoder-optimization ]
}

record jpegxl-rs.question.which-perceptual-weight-crosses-the-cleanroom-line/2 : question {
    title "Where does the frequency weight's shape come from, now that a detection CSF is ruled out?"
    state resolved
    scope [ path "JPXL/crates/jpxl-encode-policy/src/csf.rs" ]
    question """
        Phase 6.3 established that frequency weighting drives the cover objective (butteraugli moves -16.4% to +32.4%, SSIMULACRA2 up to +4.37, against transform size's near-null) and that a Mannos-Sakrison detection CSF is the wrong curve, failing its pre-registered agreement check at 3.14x worst-bin ratio and r = 0.408. Measurement and screen agree on what the right curve looks like: far flatter at high frequency than a detection CSF, with no low-frequency dip.

        The obvious next step -- give the weight the measured shape -- crosses the line the workspace manifest draws around the butteraugli dependency: a table fitted to that metric's response makes this project's perceptual model a derivative of libjxl's rather than its own, with licence and provenance consequences. AGENTS.md section 2 makes that a decision to record before it is taken, not an implementation detail.

        Three options, in the preference order Phase 6.3 recorded:

        1. A distortion-visibility model from open literature rather than a detection CSF. Same clean-room standing as Phase 6.3, and the screen has now characterised what it must look like, so the search is well-posed rather than open-ended. Keeps the perceptual model this project's own.

        2. A two-parameter analytic shape -- low-frequency plateau plus a gentle rolloff -- whose parameters are set from the encoder's own quantization geometry (the I.2.5 default matrices, whose per-size step ratios Phase 6.2 already measured) rather than from butteraugli, then checked against the Phase 6.2 measurement the same way Phase 6.3 checked Mannos-Sakrison.

        3. Fit the measured curve, explicitly recorded as a decision that the perceptual model becomes libjxl-derived.

        A fourth possibility the rate split raises: the 1 bpp arm is already a uniform SSIMULACRA2 win and a 5-of-7 butteraugli win, and the 2 bpp arm is where it fails. That is evidence the correct weight interacts with which coefficients survive quantization -- something no static table can capture, and a pointer at HfQuantizer::choose, which is still nearest-reconstruction rather than RD-optimal. The next round may belong on the quantizer rather than on a better table.
        """
    resolution """
        Resolved by Phase 6.5 without crossing the line.

        Option 1 is closed. The canonical DCT-domain per-coefficient models -- Ahumada & Peterson (1992), Peterson, Ahumada & Watson (1993), Watson's DCTune (1993), Nill (1985) -- are all detection-threshold models: band-pass with a low-frequency dip and a high-frequency rolloff HARDER than Mannos-Sakrison. AP92 predicts about 0.037 of peak sensitivity at Nyquist where measurement says about 1.05, roughly 28x off against Mannos-Sakrison's 3.1x, so adopting any of them would be strictly worse than what Phase 6.3 rejected. Georgeson & Sullivan (1975) explain why: above threshold, perceived contrast becomes largely independent of spatial frequency (contrast constancy), so a detection CSF -- which describes seeing a grating on a blank field -- is the wrong regime for judging quantization error in real content, where low-frequency error is highly visible.

        Option 2 supplies the answer, in corrected form. The shape comes from the standard's own I.2.5 DCT8x8 matrix, read as a curve in normalised radial frequency and resampled onto every transform's grid -- NOT from each size's own matrix, which is the 1/step^2 weighting Phase 6.2 falsified, because the matrices are per-size tuned and disagree 3.34x across sizes. It scores median 1.41x against the measured target, against the CSF's 2.06x and the 1.18x ceiling of a curve fitted directly to butteraugli.

        Option 3 is declined. The remaining 1.41x-to-1.18x margin does not justify making the perceptual model libjxl-derived. The model stays this project's own, sourced from the standard.

        The fourth possibility this record raised -- that the right weight interacts with which coefficients survive quantization -- is now supported rather than speculative: Phase 6.5's donor is a large win at 0.5 bpp, clear at 1 bpp, neutral at 2 and a no-op at 4 bpp. Carried forward in jpegxl-rs.observation.donor-weight-wins-at-low-rate-and-fades-2026-08-12, whose conclusion is that the next round belongs on HfQuantizer::choose, still nearest-reconstruction rather than RD-optimal.
        """
    blocks [ @jpegxl-rs.work.arch-phase6-4-chroma-controlled-operating-point ]
    derived_from [
        @jpegxl-rs.observation.frequency-is-the-live-lever-csf-is-the-wrong-curve-2026-08-12
    ]
    part_of [ @jpegxl-rs.track.encoder-optimization ]
    supersedes [ @jpegxl-rs.question.which-perceptual-weight-crosses-the-cleanroom-line/1 ]
}
